---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../config/site';
---

<Base title="The Valid JSON Checklist" description="A practical checklist for writing JSON that parses first time — and for finding the bug fast when it does not." path="/guides/valid-json-checklist/">
  <nav class="breadcrumb"><a href={withBase('/')}>home</a> / <a href={withBase('/guides/')}>guides</a> / <span>valid-json-checklist</span></nav>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>The valid JSON checklist: write it right, debug it fast</h1>
    <p>
      JSON looks forgiving because it resembles JavaScript, but the grammar is much stricter
      than the language it came from. Most "invalid JSON" bugs are not mysterious — they are
      one of about seven recurring mistakes. Run through this checklist when you write JSON
      by hand, and again when a parser rejects a payload you received from somewhere else.
    </p>

    <h2>The seven rules that catch almost everything</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>1. Keys are always double-quoted strings.</strong> In JSON there is no such
      thing as an unquoted key or a single-quoted string. If you write a key without quotes,
      or wrap a value in single quotes because that is what your programming language prefers,
      a strict parser will refuse the document at that exact character.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>2. No trailing commas.</strong> JavaScript arrays and objects tolerate a comma
      after the last item; JSON does not. Trailing commas are the single most common cause of
      hand-edited config files failing to load, precisely because they look harmless.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>3. Strings escape a small, fixed set of characters.</strong> Inside a string,
      a double quote must be written as \" and a backslash as \\, and control characters such
      as newlines must be escaped as \n — never typed literally. Text copied out of a log file
      or an email frequently breaks here.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>4. Numbers have one format.</strong> No leading plus, no leading zeros (0.5 is
      fine, 00.5 is not), no hexadecimal, and no bare NaN or Infinity — those are JavaScript
      values, not JSON. Scientific notation such as 1.2e3 is allowed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>5. The only literals are true, false and null — lowercase.</strong> True,
      False, None and NULL all belong to other languages. JSON is case-sensitive about its
      three literal values, and parsers will not guess what you meant.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>6. Comments do not exist.</strong> There is no // or /* */ syntax in JSON. If
      you need annotations in a config file, that format is JSON5, JSONC or YAML — not JSON —
      and a strict parser will fail on the first comment it meets.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>7. The document is one value, encoded as UTF-8.</strong> A JSON file contains
      exactly one top-level value: an object, array, string, number, boolean or null. Two
      objects in a row is a JSON Lines stream, not a JSON document, and stray text after the
      closing bracket — a log prefix, a BOM gone wrong — invalidates the whole thing.
    </p>

    <h2>Debugging a payload that will not parse</h2>
    <p>
      Start from the error position, not from the top of the file. Parsers stop at the first
      character they cannot make sense of, so the real mistake is almost always at, or a few
      characters before, the reported line and column. Look backwards from that point for the
      usual suspects: the comma after the previous item, the quote that was supposed to close
      the previous string, the bracket that never opened.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, format what you have. If most of the document survives reformatting in a tool
      like the TextKit <a href={withBase('/tool/json-formatter/')}>JSON Formatter</a>, the
      broken region stands out visually — indentation suddenly stops lining up where the
      structure went wrong. Finally, shrink the problem: delete half the payload and test
      again. Two or three rounds of bisection will isolate any syntax error to a handful of
      lines, no matter how large the original file was.
    </p>
    <p>
      And a defensive habit worth adopting: whenever your code produces JSON, produce it with
      a serializer, never by joining strings. Every hand-built JSON string is a future
      escaping bug waiting for the one input that contains a quote.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
